---
import BareLayout from "../../layouts/BareLayout.astro";
import Section from "../../smalltalk/components/Section.astro";
import Win from "../../smalltalk/components/Win.astro";
import Predict from "../../smalltalk/components/Predict.astro";
import Recall from "../../smalltalk/components/Recall.astro";
import Why from "../../smalltalk/components/Why.astro";
import "@fontsource/pixelify-sans/500.css";
import "@fontsource/pixelify-sans/700.css";
import "@fontsource/atkinson-hyperlegible/400.css";
import "@fontsource/atkinson-hyperlegible/400-italic.css";
import "@fontsource/atkinson-hyperlegible/700.css";
import "@fontsource/atkinson-hyperlegible-mono/400.css";
import "@fontsource/atkinson-hyperlegible-mono/500.css";
import "../../smalltalk/style.css";
---

<BareLayout
  title="Smalltalk, One Message at a Time"
  edgeToEdge
  description="An interactive Smalltalk-80 tutorial: send messages yourself, starting with Hello World and going down to the bytecodes a VM runs."
>
<svg width="0" height="0" style="position:absolute" aria-hidden="true"><defs>
  <pattern id="svgD12" width="4" height="4" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse"><rect width="4" height="4" style="fill:var(--paper)"/><rect width="1" height="1" style="fill:var(--ink)"/></pattern>
  <pattern id="svgHatch" width="6" height="6" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse"><rect width="6" height="6" style="fill:var(--paper)"/><path d="M0 6L6 0" style="stroke:var(--ink)" stroke-width="1"/></pattern>
  <pattern id="svgWater" width="4" height="4" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse"><rect width="4" height="4" style="fill:var(--paper)"/><rect width="1" height="1" style="fill:var(--ink)"/><rect x="2" y="2" width="1" height="1" style="fill:var(--ink)"/></pattern>
</defs></svg>

<nav id="menubar" aria-label="Page menu">
  <span class="name">Smalltalk, One Message at a Time</span>
  <button class="mb" id="mb-contents" aria-expanded="false" aria-haspopup="true">Contents</button>
  <button class="mb" id="mb-ledger" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="ledger">Built-ins: 7</button>
  <button class="mb" id="mb-reverse">Invert colours</button>
  <button class="mb" id="mb-reset">Undo</button>
</nav>
<div class="pop" id="contentsmenu" hidden></div>
<aside class="win" id="ledger" aria-label="Built into most languages"><div class="tab">Built into most languages</div><p class="ledgernote">Keywords and operators that Python, JavaScript or Java build in. As you read, each one flips to show what Smalltalk does instead.</p><ul class="ledgerrows" id="ledgerrows"></ul></aside>

<div id="page"><main class="col">

<div class="win" id="welcome" hidden><div class="pad">
  <p class="q" id="welcome-text">Welcome back.</p>
  <div class="btnrow"><button class="btn" id="welcome-quiz">Five questions</button><button class="btn" id="welcome-place">Pick up where you stopped</button></div>
</div></div>

<section id="hero" class="win" aria-labelledby="h-title">
  <div class="tab">Workspace</div>
  <div class="pad herogrid">
    <h1 id="h-title">Smalltalk, One Message at a Time</h1>
    <p class="tiny">Smalltalk-80 builds almost everything from objects sending messages to each other. This tutorial lets you run that idea yourself, starting with one send and going down to the bytecodes underneath.</p>
    <div class="bench" id="bench">
      <button class="slip heroslip" id="heroslip" aria-label="Send show: 'Hello, world' to the Transcript"><svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="1" y="3" width="14" height="1"/><rect x="1" y="12" width="14" height="1"/><rect x="1" y="3" width="1" height="10"/><rect x="14" y="3" width="1" height="10"/><rect x="3" y="5" width="1" height="1"/><rect x="4" y="6" width="1" height="1"/><rect x="5" y="7" width="1" height="1"/><rect x="6" y="8" width="1" height="1"/><rect x="7" y="9" width="2" height="1"/><rect x="9" y="8" width="1" height="1"/><rect x="10" y="7" width="1" height="1"/><rect x="11" y="6" width="1" height="1"/><rect x="12" y="5" width="1" height="1"/></svg>show: 'Hello, world'</button>
      <div class="herots" id="herots"><div class="tshead">Transcript</div><pre id="herots-text" aria-live="polite"></pre></div>
    </div>
    <p class="q" id="hero-do">Drag the message onto the Transcript.</p>
    <div class="reveal" id="hero-mid" tabindex="-1" hidden>
      <p>You ran <code>Transcript show: 'Hello, world'</code>. Transcript is an object that shows text in a window, and <code>show:</code> is a message it understands. This is Smalltalk's usual Hello World.</p>
      <div class="badge">In Python, <code>print</code> is a function you call. Here you ask an object.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="bench bench2" id="bench2" hidden>
      <div class="herocol"><div class="heroobj" id="hero-hello">'hello'</div><small id="hello-label">a String</small></div>
      <button class="slip heroslip" id="heroslip2" aria-label="Send asUppercase to 'hello'"><svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="1" y="3" width="14" height="1"/><rect x="1" y="12" width="14" height="1"/><rect x="1" y="3" width="1" height="10"/><rect x="14" y="3" width="1" height="10"/><rect x="3" y="5" width="1" height="1"/><rect x="4" y="6" width="1" height="1"/><rect x="5" y="7" width="1" height="1"/><rect x="6" y="8" width="1" height="1"/><rect x="7" y="9" width="2" height="1"/><rect x="9" y="8" width="1" height="1"/><rect x="10" y="7" width="1" height="1"/><rect x="11" y="6" width="1" height="1"/><rect x="12" y="5" width="1" height="1"/></svg>asUppercase</button>
      <div class="herocol" id="answercol" hidden><div class="heroobj" id="hero-upper">'HELLO'</div><small>the answer</small></div>
    </div>
    <p class="q" id="hero-do2" hidden>Now drag <code>asUppercase</code> onto <code>'hello'</code>.</p>
    <div class="reveal" id="hero-after" tabindex="-1" hidden>
      <p><code>'hello'</code> answered with a new object, <code>'HELLO'</code>, and <code>'hello'</code> is still <code>'hello'</code>. <span class="pg">p. 167</span> You send a message and get an object back. Nearly all of Smalltalk works this way, and the page names the exceptions as they come up.</p>
      <p class="tiny">Your language builds in keywords and operators. Built-ins in the menu bar lists seven of them, and most turn out to be messages here.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="part">Part one: Messages</div>

<Section id="s1" n={1}>
    <h2>Ask</h2>
    <p>What you just did is called a send, and <code>show: 'Hello, world'</code> is the message. The object that gets it, <code>Transcript</code>, is the receiver. <code>show:</code> is the selector, which names the request, and <code>'Hello, world'</code> is an argument.</p>
    <p>Think of a note slid under a door. Only the note gets in, and whoever is inside decides what to do with it. Unlike mail, you wait at the door for the answer. Kay pictured each object as a little computer on a network. <a href="https://worrydream.com/EarlyHistoryOfSmalltalk/">Kay 1993</a></p>
    <Predict id="s1" opts={['14', '20']} right="20" hold="Now press Step and watch.">
      <Fragment slot="q">What does <code>2 + 3 * 4</code> answer? Guess before you look.</Fragment>
        <p>20. <code>+</code> and <code>*</code> are plain messages with the same rank, so they run left to right. <span class="pg">p. 28</span></p>
        <div class="badge">Python and Java answer 14.</div>
      </Predict>
    <p>There are three kinds of message. <span class="pg">pp. 26-30</span></p>
    <dl class="kinds">
      <dt>Unary, a plain word</dt><dd><button class="chip" data-st="st1" data-src="'hello' size">'hello' size</button></dd>
      <dt>Binary, punctuation</dt><dd><button class="chip" data-st="st1" data-src="'hello' , ' world'">'hello' , ' world'</button></dd>
      <dt>Keyword, words with colons</dt><dd><button class="chip" data-st="st1" data-src="'hello' copyReplaceAll: 'l' with: 'L'">'hello' copyReplaceAll: 'l' with: 'L'</button></dd>
    </dl>
    <p>Unary messages run first, then binary, then keyword. <button class="chip" data-st="st1" data-src="'hello' size + 'world' size">'hello' size + 'world' size</button> runs both <code>size</code> sends before the <code>+</code>, and answers 10.</p>
    <div class="btnrow"><button class="chip" data-st="st1" data-src="2 + (3 * 4)">2 + (3 * 4)</button><button class="chip" data-st="st1" data-src="3 squared + 1 max: 20">3 squared + 1 max: 20</button><button class="chip" data-st="st1" data-src="'hello' , ' world' copyReplaceAll: 'o' with: '0'">'hello' , ' world' copyReplaceAll: 'o' with: '0'</button></div>
    <Why><Fragment slot="q">Why can't <code>*</code> outrank <code>+</code>?</Fragment>Anyone can define new punctuation messages. The language gives them all the same rank.</Why>
  
  <Fragment slot="stage"><Win tab="Stage: a send"><div id="st1"></div></Win></Fragment>
</Section>

<Section id="s2" n={2}>
    <h2>It decides</h2>
    <div class="recall"><p class="q">In <code>'hello' , ' world'</code>, which object receives <code>,</code>?</p>
      <div class="tokrow" id="recall2"><button class="btn mono" data-a="1">'hello'</button><button class="btn mono">,</button><button class="btn mono">' world'</button></div>
      <p class="ans" hidden><b><code>'hello'</code></b> receives it. <code>' world'</code> is the argument.</p></div>
    <Predict id="s2" opts={['Before the program runs', 'When the message arrives']} right="When the message arrives">
      <Fragment slot="q"><code>x size</code>. <code>x</code> could be a String, an Array, a Set or a Dictionary. When does Smalltalk check that <code>x</code> has a <code>size</code>?</Fragment>
        <p>When the message arrives. Tap each receiver.</p>
        <div class="badge">Java checks this at compile time. Python and JavaScript also look on arrival. In Smalltalk every operation goes through this lookup, <code>+</code> included.</div>
      </Predict>
    <p>Lookup starts in the receiver's class and moves up through superclasses. The sender never picks the method. The one exception is <code>super</code>, in section 5. <span class="pg">pp. 16, 61</span></p>
    <p>Kay's short definition of object orientation is messaging, hidden state and very late binding. <a href="http://userpage.fu-berlin.de/~ram/pub/pub_jf47ht81Ht/doc_kay_oop_en">Kay 2003</a> Those four receivers showed you the late binding.</p>
    <p class="tiny">Numbers work the same way. The stage's Expressions menu sends <code>+ 5</code> to <code>3</code>, <code>2.5</code> and <code>1@2</code>, a Point. Each runs a different <code>+</code>.</p>
    <Why><Fragment slot="q">You add a <code>Complex</code> class. What must change for its <code>+</code> to work?</Fragment><code>Complex</code> brings its own <code>+</code>, and there's no central routine to edit. Ingalls makes this point about new number classes. <a href="https://www.cs.virginia.edu/~evans/cs655/readings/smalltalk.html">Ingalls 1981</a></Why>
  
  <Fragment slot="stage"><Win tab="Stage: size, four times">
    <div class="pad" style="padding:12px 12px 0"><div class="recvrow" id="recv2"><span class="sendlbl" aria-hidden="true">send <code>size</code> to</span><button class="btn" id="r2-a" disabled>'hello'</button><button class="btn" id="r2-b" disabled>#(3 1 4 1 5)</button><button class="btn" id="r2-c" disabled>#(3 1 4 1 5) asSet</button><button class="btn" id="r2-d" disabled>opposites</button></div>
    <p class="tiny"><code>#(3 1 4 1 5)</code> is a literal Array. <code>asSet</code> makes a Set from the Array. <code>opposites</code> holds the first three pairs of the book's Dictionary, starting with <code>opposites at: #hot put: #cold</code>. <span class="pg">pp. 21, 149</span></p>
    <p class="q" id="r2-result" hidden>In this Smalltalk, three different methods ran. The Set answers 4 because it keeps one of each. The Dictionary has no <code>size</code> of its own, so the search went up to Set.</p></div>
    <div id="st2"></div></Win></Fragment>
</Section>

<Section id="s3" n={3}>
    <h2>Nobody home</h2>
    <Recall><Fragment slot="q">A class has no match. Where does the search go?</Fragment><b>its superclass</b></Recall>
    <Predict id="s3" opts={['The VM halts.', 'It answers nil.', 'Another message gets sent.']} right="Another message gets sent." hold="Now watch the stage.">
      <Fragment slot="q"><code>'hello' shout</code> reaches the top with no match. What happens next?</Fragment><p>The system wraps your message in a Message object and sends <code>doesNotUnderstand:</code> to the same receiver. Object's method for it opens the notifier on the stage. <span class="pg">pp. 61, 589</span></p></Predict>
    <p class="q">Give String the method. Press Create, write <code>shout ^ self asUppercase , '!'</code>, then Accept and Proceed.</p>
    <button class="btn mono" id="try3" style="align-self:flex-start" disabled>'hello' shout</button>
    <div class="reveal" id="s3done" hidden>
      <p>The program kept its place while you wrote the method, then carried on from there.</p>
      <p>Symbol is a subclass of String, so a Symbol finds your method too. <span class="pg">p. 168</span></p>
      <button class="btn mono" id="try3b" style="align-self:flex-start">#hello shout</button>
      <p class="tiny">Try a number too. Run <code>3 double</code>, and put the method on Number so that <code>2.5 double</code> finds it as well. Tap Number in the tower before you accept.</p>
      <div class="btnrow"><button class="btn mono sm" id="try3c">3 double</button><button class="btn mono sm" id="try3d">2.5 double</button></div>
    </div>
    <Why><Fragment slot="q">A class can override <code>doesNotUnderstand:</code>. What could you build?</Fragment>A stand-in that logs each message or forwards it to another machine. <a href="https://smalltalkzoo.computerhistory.org/papers/EvolutionOfSmalltalk.pdf">Ingalls 2020</a>, <a href="https://book.gtoolkit.com/overriding-doesnotunderstand--9x2unsyac7r92r0p3bligpxyk">GToolkit</a></Why>
  
  <Fragment slot="stage"><Win tab="Stage: no match"><div id="st3"></div></Win></Fragment>
</Section>
<p class="stopnote" id="stop1">You can stop here. <span class="savedline">Your place is saved in this browser.</span></p>

<div class="part">Part two: Control flow and classes</div>

<Section id="s4" n={4}>
    <h2>No <code class="h2code">if</code>, no <code class="h2code">for</code></h2>
    <Recall><Fragment slot="q">Which message reaches an object when lookup fails?</Fragment><b class="m">doesNotUnderstand:</b></Recall>
    <p>A block is code in brackets. It waits until it receives <code>value</code>. <span class="pg">p. 31</span> Try <button class="chip" data-st="st4" data-src="['hello' asUppercase]">['hello' asUppercase]</button> and <button class="chip" data-st="st4" data-src="['hello' asUppercase] value">['hello' asUppercase] value</button>.</p>
    <Predict id="s4" opts={['The compiler', 'The block', 'The object that came back']} right="The object that came back">
      <Fragment slot="q"><code>3 &gt; 0</code> answers an object, and there is no <code>if</code> keyword. What decides whether the block runs?</Fragment><p>The object that came back. <code>true</code> and <code>false</code> are ordinary objects, so give each the method it needs.</p></Predict>
    <div class="reveal" id="s4reveal" hidden>
      <p>True runs the block and False ignores it, so ordinary lookup has given you a conditional. <span class="pg">p. 239</span> Nygard calls this his favourite bit of language design. <a href="https://michaelnygard.com/blog/2018/12/my-favorite-bit-of-language-design/">Nygard 2018</a></p>
      <p>The labels on the stage are the same five steps as any other send: find receiver, evaluate arguments, look up, run, answer.</p>
      <div class="badge">In Java, <code>if (x) &#123;...&#125;</code> is syntax. Here, <code>x ifTrue: [...]</code> is a message.</div>
      <p class="honest">The real compiler turns this send into a jump for speed. You can still read it as a send, since it behaves the same for <code>true</code> and <code>false</code>. <span class="pg">pp. 550, 559</span></p>
      <p>Loops are messages too. <button class="chip" data-st="st4" data-src="x := 0. [x < 3] whileTrue: [x := x + 1]. x">[x &lt; 3] whileTrue: [x := x + 1]</button></p>
      <p>There is no <code>for</code> either. You hand a block to a collection, and the collection runs it once per element. <span class="pg">pp. 136-139</span></p>
      <p><code>[:x | ...]</code> is a block that takes one argument, like <code>lambda x: ...</code> or <code>x =&gt; ...</code>. A <code>;</code> sends another message to the same receiver, so <code>Transcript print: x; cr</code> is two sends to Transcript. <span class="pg">pp. 30, 35</span></p>
      <div class="btnrow">
        <button class="chip" data-st="st4" data-src="#(3 1 4 1 5) do: [:x | Transcript print: x; cr]">#(3 1 4 1 5) do: [:x | Transcript print: x; cr]</button>
        <button class="chip" data-st="st4" data-src="#(3 1 4 1 5) collect: [:x | x * x]">#(3 1 4 1 5) collect: [:x | x * x]</button>
        <button class="chip" data-st="st4" data-src="#(3 1 4 1 5) select: [:x | x > 2]">#(3 1 4 1 5) select: [:x | x &gt; 2]</button>
        <button class="chip" data-st="st4" data-src="#(3 1 4 1 5) inject: 0 into: [:sum :x | sum + x]">#(3 1 4 1 5) inject: 0 into: [:sum :x | sum + x]</button>
        <button class="chip" data-st="st4" data-src="'hello' select: [:c | c isVowel]">'hello' select: [:c | c isVowel]</button>
      </div>
      <p><code>collect:</code> and <code>select:</code> answer a new collection of the receiver's kind, so the Array gives an Array and the String gives a String. <span class="pg">p. 136</span> An Array prints as <code>(9 1 16 1 25 )</code>, without its class name. <span class="pg">pp. 218-219</span></p>
      <div class="badge">In Python, <code>for x in items:</code> is syntax. Here, <code>items do: [:x | ...]</code> is a message to the collection.</div>
      <p>Counting is a message to a number: <button class="chip" data-st="st4" data-src="1 to: 3 do: [:i | Transcript print: i; cr]">1 to: 3 do: [:i | Transcript print: i; cr]</button></p>
    </div>
    <Why><Fragment slot="q">Why must the argument be a block?</Fragment>Arguments are evaluated before the send. A block delays its code so the receiver can choose.</Why>
  
  <Fragment slot="stage">
    <Win tab="Puzzle: two methods">
      <p class="tiny" id="puzzle-wait" style="padding:14px 12px">Two empty methods wait here. Make your guess first.</p>
      <div class="puzzle" id="puzzle" hidden>
      <p class="tiny"><code>True &gt;&gt; ifTrue:</code> means the <code>ifTrue:</code> method in class True. <code>^</code> means answer this.</p>
      <div class="pmethods">
        <div class="pmeth"><header>True &gt;&gt; ifTrue: aBlock</header><button class="pslot" id="slot-true" aria-label="Slot in True, ifTrue:"></button></div>
        <div class="pmeth"><header>False &gt;&gt; ifTrue: aBlock</header><button class="pslot" id="slot-false" aria-label="Slot in False, ifTrue:"></button></div>
      </div>
      <div class="ptiles"><button class="ptile" id="tile-value">^ aBlock value</button><button class="ptile" id="tile-nil">^ nil</button></div>
      <div class="btnrow"><button class="btn mono sm" id="runp1" disabled>3 &gt; 0 ifTrue: ['yes']</button><button class="btn mono sm" id="runp2" disabled>3 &lt; 0 ifTrue: ['yes']</button></div>
      <p class="tiny" id="pmsg" hidden>That ran backwards. Swap them and run again.</p>
      <button class="link sm" id="phint" hidden>One of them runs the block. One doesn't.</button>
    </div></Win>
    <Win tab="Stage: blocks"><div id="st4"></div></Win>
  </Fragment>
</Section>

<Section id="s5" n={5}>
    <h2>Who is self</h2>
    <Recall><Fragment slot="q">What does <code>false</code> answer to <code>ifTrue:</code>?</Fragment><b class="m">nil</b></Recall>
    <p><code>self</code> and <code>super</code> both mean the receiver. They differ in where the search starts. <code>self</code> starts in the receiver's own class. <code>super</code> starts above the class holding the running method. <span class="pg">pp. 62-65</span></p>
    <div class="badge">This is close to <code>this</code> and <code>super</code> in Java, or <code>self</code> and <code>super()</code> in Python. Nothing checks these sends in advance, though.</div>
    <div class="btnrow"><button class="btn mono sm" id="w5a">example4 result1</button><button class="btn mono sm" id="w5b">example4 result2</button></div>
    <p class="tiny">Both answer 4. <span class="pg">p. 64</span> Step through each one.</p>
    <Predict id="s5" opts={['2', '4']} right="2" hold="Now press Step and watch.">
      <Fragment slot="q">A Four runs <code>result3</code>, which says <code>super test</code>. What comes back?</Fragment><p>2, the number. <code>result3</code> lives in Three, so <code>super</code> starts at Two, and Four's <code>test</code> is never seen. <span class="pg">pp. 64-65</span></p></Predict>
    <Why><Fragment slot="q">Why start from the method's class and not the receiver's?</Fragment>Starting from the receiver's class could find the same method again and loop forever. <i>We worked this out. The book doesn't say.</i></Why>
  
  <Fragment slot="stage">
    <Win tab="The four classes"><div class="fourcls">
      <div><b>One</b><code>test ^ 1</code><code>result1 ^ self test</code></div>
      <div><b>Two, under One</b><code>test ^ 2</code></div>
      <div><b>Three, under Two</b><code>result2 ^ self result1</code><code>result3 ^ super test</code></div>
      <div><b>Four, under Three</b><code>test ^ 4</code></div>
    </div><p class="tiny" style="padding:0 12px 12px"><code>example4</code> is a Four. <span class="pg">pp. 62-64</span></p></Win>
    <Win tab="Stage: One, Two, Three, Four"><div id="st5"></div></Win>
  </Fragment>
</Section>

<Section id="s6" n={6}>
    <h2>Classes answer too</h2>
    <div class="btnrow"><span class="tiny">Optional on a first pass.</span><a class="btn" id="skip6" href="#s7" style="display:inline-flex;align-items:center;text-decoration:none">Skip</a></div>
    <Recall><Fragment slot="q">Where does <code>super</code> start?</Fragment><b>above the class holding the running method</b></Recall>
    <Predict id="s6" opts={['You reach nil.', 'A new class every time, forever.', 'You come back to one you already visited.']} right="You come back to one you already visited.">
      <Fragment slot="q">Send <code>class</code> to <code>3</code>, then to the answer, again and again. What happens?</Fragment><p>You come back around. Metaclass class is an instance of Metaclass. <span class="pg">p. 270</span> In Python, <code>type(type) is type</code> is the same kind of loop.</p></Predict>
    <p>Making an object is a send too. <button class="chip" data-st="st6" data-src="OrderedCollection new">OrderedCollection new</button> asks the class OrderedCollection for a new instance, which you can then fill: <button class="chip" data-st="st6" data-src="OrderedCollection new add: 3; add: 4; yourself">OrderedCollection new add: 3; add: 4; yourself</button></p>
    <p class="tiny"><code>add:</code> answers the thing added, not the collection, so <code>yourself</code> at the end asks for the collection back. <span class="pg">p. 218</span></p>
    <p>And you can ask any object for its class: <button class="chip" data-st="st6" data-src="'hello' class">'hello' class</button> answers String, an object that receives messages like any other.</p>
    <p>A class is an object, so it has a class too, called its metaclass. Methods on the class itself live there. This is why <code>Point</code> answers <code>x:y:</code> and <code>3</code> does not. <span class="pg">pp. 76-79, 269</span></p>
    <div class="badge"><code>OrderedCollection new</code> and <code>Point x: 1 y: 2</code> look like Java's <code>new</code> and a static call. Both are ordinary sends to an object, so they can be inherited and overridden.</div>
    <Why><Fragment slot="q">Why one metaclass per class?</Fragment>Classes need different messages. When all classes shared one, they all had one protocol. <span class="pg">pp. 76-77</span></Why>
  
  <Fragment slot="stage">
    <Win tab="Map: who is an instance of what">
      <div class="mapwrap" id="mapwrap"></div>
