<div class="part">Part three: The machine and daily habits</div>

<Section id="s7" n={7}>
    <h2>Below the objects</h2>
    <Recall><Fragment slot="q">In <code>'hello' size</code>, who picks the code for <code>size</code>?</Fragment><b>the class of <code>'hello'</code></b>, String</Recall>
    <p>Smalltalk compiles each method to bytecodes, much as Java compiles to .class files and Python to .pyc. The virtual machine (VM) is the program that runs them. The book compiles Rectangle <code>center</code> to these six bytes: three pushes, two sends and a return. The VM's loop fetches a byte, advances, and performs it. <span class="pg">pp. 543, 551</span></p>
    <p>Each method that runs gets a new context (a stack frame), an ordinary object holding sender, receiver, method, temporaries and stack. A primitive is a method the VM carries out itself, in machine code, so it needs no context. The debugger is plain Smalltalk code that reads those objects. <span class="pg">pp. 581, 609, 612</span> <a href="https://smalltalkzoo.computerhistory.org/papers/EvolutionOfSmalltalk.pdf">Ingalls 2020</a></p>
    <Predict id="s7b" opts={['Yes', 'No']} right="Yes">
      <Fragment slot="q">Can you send a message to the stack frame that's running right now?</Fragment>
        <p>Yes. Run <button class="chip" data-st="st7" data-src="thisContext receiver">thisContext receiver</button> and watch.</p>
        <p>Every stage above now has a Machine button that shows the same send from the VM's side.</p>
      </Predict>
    <Why><Fragment slot="q">Why does the sender's context stay around while a method runs?</Fragment>The answer comes back to it, and the run resumes there. Sends are synchronous.</Why>
  
  <Fragment slot="stage">
    <Win tab="Rectangle center, in bytes" id="bytewin"><div class="bytes" id="bytes"></div></Win>
    <Win tab="Stage: the same send, one level up"><div id="st7"></div></Win>
  </Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="after">
  <Predict id="s7" opts={['It adds.', 'It sends.']} right="It sends." hold="Now press Step and watch.">
      <Fragment slot="q">176 is the shortcut for <code>+</code>. Does the VM add here?</Fragment><p>It sends. The shortcut works only for a SmallInteger receiver, and this receiver is a Point. <span class="pg">worked out from pp. 618-619</span></p></Predict>
  </Fragment>
</Section>

<Section id="s8" n={8}>
    <h2>The short list</h2>
    <p>Look at your Built-ins list. <code>:=</code> and <code>^</code> stay on it. Everything else turned out to be objects and messages.</p>
    <p>Files, the display, the compiler and the debugger are objects too. Under them is a thin layer of about 100 primitives. <span class="pg">pp. 52, 543, 563</span> <a href="https://www.cs.virginia.edu/~evans/cs655/readings/smalltalk.html">Ingalls 1981</a></p>
    <div class="reveal" id="s8done" hidden><p>Ingalls wanted a system one person could understand, which is why this list is so short. <a href="https://www.cs.virginia.edu/~evans/cs655/readings/smalltalk.html">Ingalls 1981</a></p></div>
  
