<section class="win" id="tomorrow"><div class="tab">Come back tomorrow</div><div class="pad">
  <p>Try these from memory in about a day. Spacing them out helps you keep them.</p>
  <div id="quizwait" hidden><p class="q">The five questions appear here tomorrow.</p><button class="link sm" id="quiznow">Show them anyway</button></div>
  <div id="quiz" class="quiz">
    <Recall><Fragment slot="q"><code>3 + 4 * 2</code> answers what?</Fragment><b>14</b>, left to right.</Recall>
    <Recall><Fragment slot="q">What does <code>#(3 1 4) collect: [:x | x * 2]</code> answer, and what happens to <code>#(3 1 4)</code>?</Fragment><b class="m">(6 2 8 )</b>, a new Array. The original is unchanged.</Recall>
    <Recall><Fragment slot="q"><code>'hello'</code> gets a message nothing defines. What arrives next, and where?</Fragment><b class="m">doesNotUnderstand:</b>, at the same String.</Recall>
    <Recall><Fragment slot="q">A method in Three says <code>super test</code>, and the receiver is a Four. Where does the search start?</Fragment><b>Two</b>, above the class holding the method.</Recall>
    <Recall><Fragment slot="q">If you read section 6: what is <code>3 class class class</code>?</Fragment><b class="m">Metaclass</b></Recall>
  </div>
</div></section>

<details class="win fold" id="stories" open><summary>Stories</summary><div class="pad">
  <div style="position:relative"><button class="btn" id="storypick" aria-haspopup="true" aria-expanded="false">Stories: Look at the gaps &#9662;</button></div>
  <div id="story-gaps" class="story">
    <canvas id="gaps" width="320" height="200" role="img" aria-label="Twelve objects with messages passing between them"></canvas>
    <input type="range" id="gapslider" min="0" max="100" value="0" aria-label="From the things to the between">
    <div class="slideends"><span>the things</span><span>the between</span></div>
    <p>Kay later regretted the word objects, since it pulled attention off messaging. He pointed at the space between things. <a href="http://lists.squeakfoundation.org/pipermail/squeak-dev/1998-October/017019.html">Kay 1998</a></p>
  </div>
  <div id="story-bet" class="story" hidden><p>Kay claimed the most powerful language could fit on a page. Ingalls and Kaehler told Kay to show it. Kay got up at 4am for two weeks, and it just about fit. <a href="https://smalltalkzoo.computerhistory.org/papers/EvolutionOfSmalltalk.pdf">Ingalls 2020</a>, <a href="https://worrydream.com/EarlyHistoryOfSmalltalk/">Kay 1993</a></p></div>
  <div id="story-first" class="story" hidden><p>Ingalls remembers its first program as <code>6 factorial</code>. Kay remembers <code>3 + 4</code>, very slowly. <a href="https://smalltalkzoo.computerhistory.org/papers/EvolutionOfSmalltalk.pdf">Ingalls 2020</a>, <a href="https://worrydream.com/EarlyHistoryOfSmalltalk/">Kay 1993</a></p><button class="btn" id="try6" style="margin-top:8px">Try 6 factorial.</button><div id="stStory" style="margin-top:8px"></div></div>
  <div id="story-word" class="story" hidden><p>Jobs asked for smooth scrolling. Ingalls reports rearranging a couple of methods over lunch, then changing <code>delta</code> to <code>delta asFloat</code>. <a href="https://smalltalkzoo.computerhistory.org/papers/EvolutionOfSmalltalk.pdf">Ingalls 2020</a></p></div>
  <div id="story-kids" class="story" hidden><p>Marian Goldeen, 12, and Bruce Horn, 15, built a paint system and a music capture tool. <a href="https://worrydream.com/EarlyHistoryOfSmalltalk/">Kay 1993</a></p></div>
  <div id="story-off" class="story" hidden><p>Beck's Smalltalk test framework led to JUnit, first written on a flight with Erich Gamma. <a href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/Xunit.html">Fowler 2006</a> CRC cards came from passing on Smalltalk knowledge. <a href="https://www.cs.unc.edu/~stotts/COMP145/CRC/crc.html">UNC notes</a></p></div>
</div></details>

<details class="win fold" id="short"><summary>Where it falls short</summary><div class="pad">
  <ul class="plain">
    <li><p>Kay says messaging was never finished at PARC, and that treating Smalltalk as finished was a mistake. <a href="http://lists.squeakfoundation.org/pipermail/squeak-dev/1998-October/017019.html">Kay 1998</a></p></li>
    <li><p>Kay also says inheritance is hard for novices and experts, and that no child programmed in Smalltalk after Smalltalk-76. <a href="https://worrydream.com/EarlyHistoryOfSmalltalk/">Kay 1993</a></p></li>
    <li><p>The image is a saved snapshot of every live object. Code was hard to separate from that heap, so shipping was hard. With no plain source syntax, ordinary version control was hard too. <a href="https://gbracha.blogspot.com/2020/05/bits-of-history-words-of-advice.html">Bracha 2020</a> Wirfs-Brock says tools like Envy solved much of this. <a href="https://wirfs-brock.com/allen/posts/914">Wirfs-Brock 2020</a></p></li>
    <li><p>Why it lost the market is mostly a business story, and insiders disagree on it. <a href="https://gbracha.blogspot.com/2020/05/bits-of-history-words-of-advice.html">Bracha 2020</a>, <a href="https://wirfs-brock.com/allen/posts/914">Wirfs-Brock 2020</a>, <a href="https://lists.squeakfoundation.org/pipermail/beginners/2009-November/006616.html">Johnson 2009</a></p></li>
    <li><p><code>2 + 3 * 4</code> answering 20 surprises anyone who knows school arithmetic. Parsia is one of those who call it good for learners. <a href="https://lists.squeakfoundation.org/pipermail/squeak-dev/2000-June/006298.html">Parsia 2000</a></p></li>
    <li><p>Live programming wasn't unique to Smalltalk. APL and Lisp had it. <a href="https://smalltalkzoo.computerhistory.org/papers/EvolutionOfSmalltalk.pdf">Ingalls 2020</a></p></li>
  </ul>
</div></details>

<details class="win fold" id="why"><summary>The learning research behind this page</summary><div class="pad">
  <svg id="glasses" viewBox="0 -34 420 238" role="img" aria-label="A wide glass of water, a tall empty glass and a card"></svg>
  <div class="btnrow"><button class="btn" id="gl-pour">Pour.</button><button class="btn" id="gl-cover" disabled>Cover.</button><button class="btn" id="gl-lift" disabled>Lift.</button></div>
  <p>This is Bruner's test, as Kay tells it. Doing, seeing and reasoning can give different answers, which is why this page has you drag, then watch, then read. <a href="https://www.dgsiegel.net/files/refs/Kay%20-%20User%20Interface:%20A%20Personal%20View.pdf">Kay 1989</a></p>
  <ul class="plain">
    <li><p>Guessing before the answer helps, and learners rarely notice that it does. <a href="https://sc-pan.github.io/pdf/PR_2023.pdf">Pan and Rivers 2023</a></p></li>
    <li><p>Prompts to explain improve learning, hence the "why" questions. <a href="https://www.gwern.net/doc/psychology/spaced-repetition/2018-bisra.pdf">Bisra et al. 2018</a></p></li>
    <li><p>Every send uses the same five labels, following Margulieux et al. 2020.</p></li>
    <li><p>Sorva 2013 argues a working model of the machine is worth teaching outright.</p></li>
    <li><p>Threshold ideas put you in a state of uncertainty before they settle. <a href="https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10649480">Mackay and McSkimming 2024</a></p></li>
    <li><p>Most of this evidence comes from other subjects, and none of it tests Smalltalk.</p></li>
  </ul>
</div></details>

<details class="win fold" id="notes"><summary>What this little Smalltalk leaves out</summary><div class="pad">
  <p>It walks a parsed tree. Only section 7 runs real bytes, and those are a fixed table from the book.</p>
  <p>Machine view elsewhere is a model.</p>
  <p>Create, and the re-send on Proceed, are ours. The book only says the user is notified. <span class="pg">pp. 61, 589</span></p>
  <p>Conditionals are always real sends here. So <code>3 ifTrue: [...]</code> raises <code>doesNotUnderstand:</code>, where the real system sends <code>mustBeBoolean</code>.</p>
  <p>Large integers lose digits and print as floats, where the real system moves to LargePositiveInteger. <span class="pg">p. 614</span> <code>7 / 2</code> gives 3.5, with no Fractions.</p>
  <p>No radix or exponent literals, so <code>16r1F</code> and <code>1e3</code> won't parse. Inside a literal array, <code>true</code>, <code>false</code> and <code>nil</code> read as symbols.</p>
  <p><code>+</code> uses double dispatch from Beck. Smalltalk-80's own coercion is different.</p>
  <p>Blocks are closures named Block, where the book has BlockContext sharing its home's temporaries.</p>
  <p><code>whileTrue:</code> is our own code and grows the stack.</p>
  <p><code>do:</code>, <code>keysDo:</code> and <code>associationsDo:</code> are primitives here, so a trace stays short. In the real system they're Smalltalk methods. <code>collect:</code>, <code>select:</code>, <code>inject:into:</code> and <code>detect:ifNone:</code> are written in Smalltalk on top of <code>do:</code>, as the book describes.</p>
  <p>Contexts are read-only.</p>
  <p>The class tree is trimmed. There's no Bag, Interval, SortedCollection or Stream, Association sits directly under Object, and <code>subclass:instanceVariableNames:</code> is a shortened selector.</p>
  <p>Point's methods are our versions, and so are the collection methods. A Set puts small integers and characters in order of value, as a real one's hashing would, and keeps other elements in the order added. A Dictionary's <code>collect:</code> answers an OrderedCollection, where the book's answers a Bag.</p>
  <p>Results print the way the book describes. A string keeps its quotes, a symbol prints bare, so <code>#foo</code> shows <code>foo</code> <span class="pg">pp. 101, 168, 219</span>, and a collection prints its class name and elements <span class="pg">p. 218</span>. An Array prints as <code>(1 2 3 )</code>, with no class name <span class="pg">pp. 218-219</span>. Message slips on the stage keep the <code>#</code>, as you'd type it.</p>
  <p>The Transcript is a plain text pane under Object. In Smalltalk-80 it is a kind of WriteStream, and it belongs to the programming environment, which the Blue Book doesn't describe.</p>
  <p>No processes, <code>become:</code>, garbage collector or method cache.</p>
  <p>We write <code>:=</code> and <code>^</code> where the book prints arrows <span class="pg">pp. 22, 50</span>.</p>
</div></details>

